[{"category":"section","location":"#PhaseRetrieval","page":"Home","text":"Classic phase retrieval algorithms.","title":"PhaseRetrieval"},{"category":"section","location":"#Optical-propagation","page":"Home","text":"On Julia 1.10 or later, install PhaseRetrieval, OpticalPropagation 0.4, and Unitful in your environment. Pass a forward/backward propagation pair to the existing projector keyword. OpticalPropagation's fresnel2 uses the same physical grid in both directions, and its curried form accepts unitful propagation distances.\n\nThe following executable example starts with a zero phase estimate. The input is a MonoLightField2D, so wavelength, window size, and window centre are retained during the iterations:\n\nusing PhaseRetrieval, OpticalPropagation, Unitful\n\ndata = [exp(-3 * (x^2 + y^2)) * cis(0.8x^2 - 0.6y)\n        for x in range(-1, 1; length=9), y in range(-1, 1; length=8)]\ntruth = MonoLightField2D(data; wavelength=532u\"nm\",\n                        size=(0.8u\"mm\", 0.6u\"mm\"),\n                        center=(0.2u\"mm\", -0.1u\"mm\"))\nforward, backward = fresnel2(20u\"mm\"), fresnel2(-20u\"mm\")\nmeasured = forward(truth)\nrecovered, output = gerchbergsaxton(abs.(truth), abs.(measured);\n    projector=(forward, backward),\n    init=MonoLightField2D(truth; data=abs.(parent(truth))),\n    maxiter=30)\n\n@assert physicalsize(recovered) == physicalsize(truth)\n@assert gridcenter(recovered) == gridcenter(truth)\n@assert abs.(parent(recovered)) ≈ abs.(parent(truth))\n\nUse matching sampling grids for the amplitude measurements and propagators. The two returned fields satisfy their respective amplitude constraints; at a finite iteration count they need not be an exact forward-propagated pair. OpticalPropagation and Unitful are test/example dependencies, so the default FFT solver only loads FFTW and Distances.","title":"Optical propagation"},{"category":"section","location":"#API","page":"Home","text":"","title":"API"},{"category":"method","location":"#PhaseRetrieval.gerchbergsaxton-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Home","text":"gerchbergsaxton(Ain, Aout, kw...)\n\nreconstruct the complex signals from their amplitude values based on the Gerchberg-Saxton algorithm.\n\nReferences\n\nR. W. Gerchberg and W. O. Saxton, \"A practical algorithm for the determination of the phase from image and diffraction plane pictures,\" Optik 35, 237 (1972)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PhaseRetrieval.gerchbergsaxton"}]
